  "limitations": [
    "Small sample size (N=49 overall, N=21 for actigraphy subset).",
    "Convenience sample, which may have introduced bias.",
    "No data on caffeine use, stress levels, or chronotype, which could influence outcome measures.",
    "Actigraphy has low specificity for detecting wakefulness within sleep periods.",
    "Actigraphy is not sensitive to low light levels and not calibrated for artificial fluorescent lighting, potentially underestimating light exposure in windowless settings.",
    "Wrist actigraphy placement may introduce error by covering the actiwatch, not fully representing light levels reaching the retina.",
    "Data collection methods did not differentiate between natural daylight and artificial lighting.",
    "Data collection methods did not allow for analysis of specific wavelengths of light exposure.",
    "The study did not account for additional benefits of workplaces with windows, such as the roles of views and other dimensions."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This pilot case-control study investigated the impact of windows and daylight exposure on office workers' health and sleep quality. Workers in windowless environments reported poorer overall sleep quality, more sleep disturbances, and lower vitality and role limitation due to physical problems compared to those with windows. Actigraphy data showed workers with windows had more light exposure during work hours and evenings, and longer sleep duration during workweek and free nights, along with a trend for more physical activity. Limitations include a small convenience sample, lack of data on confounding factors like caffeine or chronotype, and limitations of wrist actigraphy for light measurement and sleep staging.",
